# The settings every bot shares -- one file, in shared/, instead of the same
# lines in five. Copy it to shared/.env and fill in what you use.
#
# Each bot also reads its own .env, next to its bot.py, and what is there wins
# over this file; what is set in the real environment (Railway's variables, a
# shell) wins over both. So a setting all five need goes here once, and a bot
# that needs it different says so in its own. shared/envfiles.py does the
# reading; the owner, 2026-09-28: "Locals can override shared."
#
# One bot's settings do not belong here and are ignored if they are: its
# schema (DB_SCHEMA), its name on the family bus (FAMILY_BOT_ID,
# FAMILY_LABEL), its policy links (PRIVACY_URL, TERMS_URL) and its payment
# tokens (PAYMENT_PROVIDER_TOKEN_*) -- set here they would apply to all five.

# --- the family database ---
# ONE Postgres for the whole family, one schema per bot. Everything that
# binds a bot to a place is this single line: point it at the cloud and the
# bot runs in the cloud, point it back at localhost and it runs here.
#
# Supabase (the family's cloud database). Copy the SESSION POOLER string
# from Project Settings -> Database -- the one on port 5432:
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
#   NOT the transaction pooler on port 6543. This bot passes its
#   search_path as a connection option, and transaction pooling throws
#   per-connection options away between transactions -- the bot would read
#   and write "public" instead of its own schema and go on heartbeating as
#   if nothing were wrong. db.py warns at startup if it sees port 6543.
#
#   NOT the direct connection (db.<ref>.supabase.co) either, unless you
#   know you have IPv6: Supabase made that one IPv6-only.
#
#   PERCENT-ENCODE the password if it contains @ : / ? # or a space
#   (@ -> %40, : -> %3A, / -> %2F, # -> %23, space -> %20). An unencoded
#   "@" splits the URL in the wrong place, and the error you get names a
#   hostname you have never heard of instead of saying "bad password".
#   And if it contains "#", quote the whole value here -- python-dotenv
#   treats an unquoted # as the start of a comment.
#
# Local dev: docker compose up -d in the REPO ROOT (one Postgres for the
# whole family), or in this folder for a standalone one.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/botfamily
#
# Only run a given bot in ONE place at a time. Two processes polling
# getUpdates with the same token means Telegram answers one of them with
# 409 Conflict; stop it here before you start it there.

# The other end, for db_backup.ps1 / family_db.py when they move data
# between two databases. Same session-pooler string as above when the cloud
# copy is Supabase.
# CLOUD_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

SIBLING_BOTS=stickerbot:StickerBot:YourStickerBotUsername,convertbot:ConvertBot:YourConvertBotUsername,downloaderbot:DownloaderBot:YourDownloaderBotUsername,anonbot:AnonBot:YourAnonBotUsername

# --- who runs these bots, for /privacy and /terms ---
# The bot holds personal data from the first message -- at minimum a numeric
# Telegram user id, which is the only thing it can address a person by -- so
# /privacy and /terms exist to say what it keeps and what it may be used for,
# and /deletemydata erases it again. All three work with none of the three
# variables below set; these only fill in the lines that name a human.
#
# A person or an address to complain to. A privacy notice that cannot name
# somebody to ask is a notice about nobody. Left blank, the line is omitted
# rather than printed empty.
OPERATOR_CONTACT=mukhtorovmurodbek@gmail.com

# --- files over the Bot API's limits (shared/big_files.py) ---
# The cloud Bot API lets a bot download 20 MB and send back 50. With these two
# set, ConvertBot and DownloaderBot move anything bigger over MTProto instead,
# in private chats. They are the Telegram *app's* credentials, not a bot's --
# my.telegram.org -> API development tools -- so one pair serves every bot. A
# bot's own CBOT_API_ID / DBOT_API_ID (in its .env) wins over them.
TELEGRAM_API_ID=
TELEGRAM_API_HASH=

# --- the donation nudge ---
# Usage-based and finite: shown after 15 successful actions, then 60 more,
# then 200 more, then never again -- and never at all to anyone who has
# already donated. See the block above DONATION_STEPS in shared_features.py
# for why the old "or every 14 days" half was removed.
#
# Off by default: pin the nudge in the chat instead of leaving it in the
# scroll. It reads well in theory ("ask once, stay visible") and less well in
# practice -- a pin is a permanent claim on the top of someone's chat for
# something explicitly voluntary, it posts a "pinned a message" service line
# of its own, and it argues with anything the bot might later want to pin for
# a real reason. With the schedule above capping the ask at three times ever,
# there is not much left for it to solve. Turn it on with `on` if you
# disagree; it pins silently and unpins for good the moment that person
# donates.
# DONATION_PIN=off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running cost. Every value below has a sensible default -- this block exists
# so you know which dials there are, not because anything needs setting.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- the database connection pool ---
# One warm connection is held open and handed to every query; more are opened
# only while several things happen at once, and handed back after
# DB_POOL_MAX_IDLE seconds. Raising DB_POOL_MAX costs the database one
# reserved backend (and its memory) per connection, so leave it low unless
# this bot is genuinely busy.
# DB_POOL_MIN=1
# DB_POOL_MAX=3
# DB_POOL_MAX_IDLE=120
# DB_POOL_TIMEOUT=15
#
# How long a pooled connection may sit unused before it is worth a round trip
# proving it is still alive on checkout. This check used to run on EVERY
# checkout, including one half a second after the last -- a whole round trip
# to the database, which on a link to another continent was a third of the
# cost of every read. Lower it if a network in between drops idle
# connections faster than this; 0 checks every time, as it used to.
# DB_POOL_CHECK_AFTER_IDLE=45

# --- Telegram long polling ---
# Seconds to hold each getUpdates open. Higher is *cheaper*, not slower:
# Telegram answers the instant an update exists, so a longer window means
# fewer HTTP requests for identical latency. 30 is comfortably inside every
# proxy's idle timeout.
# POLL_TIMEOUT=30

# --- worker threads ---
# The pool that blocking work (database calls, ffmpeg, image processing) runs
# on. Python's default sizes itself to the *host's* core count, which on a
# shared cloud host is far more than this container's share.
# WORKER_THREADS=4

# --- active-user counting ---
# /status counts distinct users over a window, so recording each user once per
# flush window is exactly as accurate as recording every update, and writes a
# fraction as much. Rows older than the retention window are pruned by the
# housekeeping job -- they cost storage and answer nothing.
# ACTIVITY_FLUSH_SECONDS=60
# ACTIVITY_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# --- in-memory per-user state ---
# python-telegram-bot never evicts its per-user cache. Anyone idle this long
# has theirs dropped; the only thing lost is a cached language, re-read from
# the database on their next message.
# USER_DATA_TTL_HOURS=12

# --- logging ---
# Rotating bot.log/errors.log files next to bot.py. On by default locally,
# off by default on Railway -- there the filesystem is ephemeral and the
# platform already captures stdout, so the files are pure disk churn.
# LOG_TO_FILES=1

# --- the family bus (see family_link.py) ---
# FAMILY_BUS=off drops this bot back to standalone: no heartbeats, no
# ManagerBot, no remote commands. Useful for isolating a problem, not as a
# permanent setting.
# FAMILY_BUS=on
# FAMILY_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=30
# The command queue is polled, fast right after the bus was last busy
# and slow once it has gone quiet -- see family_link.py. Lower idle =
# snappier /run and /ping when nothing else is going on, at a few more
# background queries a minute.
# FAMILY_BUS_POLL_ACTIVE_SECONDS=1
# FAMILY_BUS_POLL_IDLE_SECONDS=10
# FAMILY_BUS_ACTIVE_WINDOW_SECONDS=20
# How often this bot deletes its own finished rows from the shared tables,
# and how long they are kept. family.commands carries file payloads, so this
# is the difference between a database that grows forever and one that
# doesn't.
# FAMILY_HOUSEKEEPING_SECONDS=21600
# FAMILY_COMMAND_RETENTION_HOURS=24
# FAMILY_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# How long the usage samples are kept. One small row per bot per window, and
# the only thing here anybody will want to look *back* through, so it outlives
# the rest: 45 days is long enough to compare this month with last.
# FAMILY_USAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=45

# --- credit (the same on every bot, ManagerBot included) ---
# What a Stars payment buys. Each bot prices a payment made in it from its own
# environment, so these must match across the family. TERMS.md promises the
# defaults; see DEPLOY.md before changing one.
# FAMILY_TOPUP_MULTIPLIER=2
# FAMILY_TOPUP_LADDER=500:3,500:2
# FAMILY_BONUS_EXPIRY_DAYS=90
#
# What a job is sold for (pricing.py): its cost plus a markup that is the cost
# itself up to FAMILY_PRICE_KNEE ⚡, and grows as cost^FAMILY_PRICE_POWER after
# it; never under FAMILY_PRICE_MINIMUM. The cost is valued at the deepest
# rate above, so changing the ladder changes the prices with it.
# tests/test_pricing.py holds any values to covering the machine.
# FAMILY_PRICE_OVERHEAD=0.5
# FAMILY_PRICE_KNEE=5
# FAMILY_PRICE_POWER=0.8
# FAMILY_PRICE_MINIMUM=3
# FAMILY_USD_PER_STAR=0.013
#
# 1 credits Stars payments without charging for them. TestBot ONLY -- never
# set this on a bot real people pay.
# FAMILY_STARS_SANDBOX=
#
# Problem reports. "Report the issue" under an error message stores a report
# in family.problem_reports and messages it to this Telegram user id; if that
# account never started this bot, it goes out as a warning event instead,
# which ManagerBot forwards. off hides the button; the codes stay.
# FAMILY_REPORTS_TO=8796896653
# FAMILY_REPORT_BUTTONS=on

# --- what this bot costs, over time ---
# /status says what the process is using right now, which on its own tells
# nobody whether right now is unusual. This writes one row per window into
# family.usage_samples -- updates handled, distinct people, and four numbers
# read from the kernel -- and raises an event when a window stops looking
# normal. ManagerBot's /usage reads it back. Counts only: no user ids, no chat
# ids, no message text.
# USAGE_SAMPLE_MINUTES=15
#
# How full the container has to get before it is worth being told. 0.85 rather
# than 0.95 so the message arrives before the out-of-memory kill, not with it.
# USAGE_MEMORY_WARN_RATIO=0.85
#
# A window is a spike when it is this many times the median of the recent ones
# AND clears the floor. The floor is what stops "2 updates, then 12" being an
# incident on a quiet bot, which on a quiet bot is most of the time.
# USAGE_SPIKE_FACTOR=6
# USAGE_SPIKE_FLOOR=60
#
# Distinct people in 30 days, past which the owner is told once a day. Not a
# limit and nothing is refused -- it is the number that means "the smallest
# plan that fits may not be the smallest plan that fits any more".
# USAGE_MONTHLY_USERS_WARN=400

# --- flood control (shared_features.py, every public bot) ---
# A ceiling on how many updates one person may cost the bot in a rolling
# minute, checked before any handler runs. Telegram limits what a bot SENDS
# and nothing about what it receives, so without this one script can keep a
# container and a shared database busy for as long as it likes. Set high
# enough that a fast typist, an album and a run of button taps all pass --
# it is aimed at a loop, not at a hurry. 0 turns it off; the owner
# (*_ADMIN_ID) is always exempt.
# FLOOD_UPDATES_PER_MINUTE=40
#
# How often somebody over the ceiling is told so. A gate that answers every
# refused message turns an incoming flood into an outgoing one.
# FLOOD_REMIND_SECONDS=60

# --- how loudly a problem is answered ---
# Every problem a bot shows anybody is recorded (family.problem_reports), and
# problems.level() decides which of three it is: urgent, fault, or a refusal
# the bot was right to make. Urgent ones are put on the family event bus the
# moment they happen and ManagerBot forwards them; everything else waits for
# the nightly report.
#
# How long occurrences wait before being written out. They are buffered rather
# than written on the sending path, because a coded message is sent from
# inside a failure and a database round trip there adds the database to the
# list of things that can go wrong while something already has.
# PROBLEM_FLUSH_SECONDS=20
#
# At most one alert per code per this many seconds. A crash loop is one fault,
# not four hundred; what the cooldown swallows is counted and named in the
# next alert for that code.
# PROBLEM_ALERT_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=900
#
# ...and an ordinary fault is promoted to an urgent alert when the same code
# happens this many times inside this window. Fifty of one failure in an hour
# is a route that has gone down rather than fifty people having bad luck.
# Per process, so a redeploy forgets it.
# PROBLEM_BURST_COUNT=15
# PROBLEM_BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
#
# Two clocks, because the two halves of a row are different things. The
# details somebody volunteered (their id, @username, language, chat kind) are
# cleared after the first; /deletemydata clears them at once regardless. The
# record of the error itself -- which is about the software, not about
# anybody -- is deleted after the second.
# PROBLEM_DETAIL_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# PROBLEM_RETENTION_DAYS=180

# --- /feedback (shared_features.py, every public bot) ---
# What somebody sends with /feedback is kept in family.feedback -- the words,
# the bot, the language, the time, and nothing about who wrote them -- until
# ManagerBot delivers it, and deleted after this many days either way.
# FEEDBACK_RETENTION_DAYS=30
#
# How many one person may send in a day; past it they are told to try
# tomorrow. In memory, so a redeploy forgets it. 0 turns the limit off.
# FEEDBACK_PER_DAY=5

# --- every bot's own settings, all here (optional) ---
# If you would rather keep everything in one file, the bots' own settings can
# live here too -- each has its own prefix, so they do not collide. Anything a
# bot's own .env says still wins.
#
# The owner's numeric Telegram id for all five at once, comma-separated for
# more than one account -- the name Railway's variables and the C edition use.
# It stands in for SBOT_ADMIN_ID, CBOT_ADMIN_ID, DBOT_ADMIN_ID, ABOT_ADMIN_ID
# and MBOT_ADMIN_ID, any of which still wins for its own bot.
# ADMIN_ID=
# SBOT_TOKEN=
# SBOT_USERNAME=
# SBOT_ADMIN_ID=
# CBOT_TOKEN=
# CBOT_USERNAME=
# CBOT_ADMIN_ID=
# DBOT_TOKEN=
# DBOT_USERNAME=
# DBOT_ADMIN_ID=
# ABOT_TOKEN=
# ABOT_USERNAME=
# ABOT_ADMIN_ID=
# MBOT_TOKEN=
# MBOT_USERNAME=
# MBOT_ADMIN_ID=
